<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ma Yin</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">853–<a href="/years/930">930</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ma Yin was the founder of Ma Chu, one of the Ten Kingdoms of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. A carpenter turned soldier from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xuzhou">Xuzhou</a>, he came to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hunan">Hunan</a> with a band of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a> and became master of the region from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changsha">Changsha</a> in 896. Ruling under titles granted by the northern dynasties, he enriched his state through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tea">tea</a> trade with northern China.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ma Yin's family came from Yanling, in modern Xuchang in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henan">Henan</a>, and claimed descent from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han dynasty</a> general Ma Yuan; of his ancestors little is known beyond the names of his father Ma Yuanfeng, his grandfather and his great-grandfather. He worked as a carpenter in his youth. He then served in the army of Zhongwu Circuit stationed at Cai Prefecture against the rebel <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huang-chao">Huang Chao</a>, under the officers Sun Ru and Liu Jianfeng, and became known for his ability and bravery.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 887 Ma Yin and Liu Jianfeng marched with an army sent into Huainan Circuit, where Sun Ru seized command and made himself military governor. Sun's campaign against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yang-xingmi">Yang Xingmi</a> ended in 892 with his defeat and death near Xuan Prefecture. Liu and Ma then led 7,000 soldiers south, Liu as leader and Ma as forward commander, and the force grew to over 100,000. In 894 Ma persuaded the defenders of Longhui Pass to disband, and Liu's men, wearing their uniforms, took Tan Prefecture, the capital of Wu'an Circuit, by surprise.</p><p>When Ma Yin took command of Wu'an, he was advised by his strategist Gao Yu not to buy off his stronger neighbours but to settle the people and train his army. In 897 he held only Tan and Shao of the circuit's seven prefectures, the other five being in the hands of rebels. By the end of 899 his generals had taken Heng, Yong, Dao, Chēn and Lian, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-zhaozong-of-tang">Emperor Zhaozong of Tang</a> formally named him military governor.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>When Zhu Quanzhong ended the Tang and founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/later-liang">Later Liang</a> in 907, Ma Yin recognised the new emperor, who created him Prince of Chu. That year his general Qin Yanhui defeated an invasion sent by Yang Wo of Hongnong, and in 908 Ma took Lang and Li Prefectures. After the fall of the Later Liang he sent tribute to the Later Tang, whose Emperor Mingzong gave him the title King of Chu in 927; Ma then called his residence a palace and set up offices on the imperial model.</p><p>At Gao Yu's suggestion Ma Yin drew merchants by collecting no transaction taxes and coined money of iron and lead, which could only be spent inside Chu, and he allowed his subjects to pay taxes in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a>, so that Chu became a major producer of silk. He had at least 35 sons, several of whom ruled Chu after him.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ma Yin” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Ma Yin</b>, a carpenter turned soldier from Xuzhou, came to Hunan with a band of mercenaries and made himself master of the region from Changsha in 896.</blockquote><a href="/state/ma-chu">Ma Chu</a></li></ul></section>
